_🎯 Functional Correctness_ | _🟠 Major_ | _⚡ Quick win_

**Fail explicitly when no configured super-admin resolves.**

If every configured login is stale—or the list is empty—the loop completes without setting a user, but the command continues with user ID `0`. This preserves the silent failure the change is intended to prevent and later persists `0` as the session user at Line 219.

<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
 if ( ! get_current_user_id() ) {
+	$resolved_admin = false;
 	foreach ( get_super_admins() as $admin_login ) {
 		$admin = get_user_by( 'login', $admin_login );
 		if ( ! $admin ) {
 			continue;
 		}

 		wp_set_current_user( $admin->ID );
+		$resolved_admin = true;
 		break;
 	}
+
+	if ( ! $resolved_admin ) {
+		\WP_CLI::error( 'No configured super-admin login resolves to an existing WordPress user.' );
+		return;
+	}
 }
```

