`Does `@wordpress/api-fetch` version 7.36.0 support AbortSignal through the apiFetch options object?`

💡 Result:

Yes, `@wordpress/api-fetch` version 7.36.0 supports AbortSignal [1]. The package passes options through to the native fetch API [2], and supports aborting requests by passing a signal property—typically derived from an AbortController—within the options object [3][4]. This functionality allows you to use the standard AbortController pattern: const controller = new AbortController; apiFetch( { path: '/wp/v2/posts', signal: controller.signal }); // To abort the request: controller.abort; In versions prior to 7.36.0, while AbortController could be used, there were sometimes issues with how the resulting AbortError was handled [5]. Version 7.36.0 specifically improved support by ensuring that an AbortError thrown by the default fetch handler can be properly caught and handled in user-land code [1][5].

