💡 Result:

The `@wordpress/api-fetch` package is designed as a wrapper around the global window.fetch API [1][2]. As of version 7.36.0, it natively supports and passes through all options available to the fetch global, which includes the signal option used for AbortController [1][3]. Regarding your query on how to integrate AbortSignal and fetchHandler: 1. Fetch Options: Because apiFetch passes through all standard fetch options, you can pass an AbortSignal instance directly in your apiFetch request options [1][2]. Example usage: const controller = new AbortController; const signal = controller.signal; apiFetch({ path: '/wp/v2/posts', signal, // Pass the AbortSignal here }).then(data => console.log(data)).catch(err => { if (err.name === 'AbortError') { console.log('Fetch aborted'); } }); // To cancel the request: controller.abort; 2. Custom Fetch Handler: The setFetchHandler method allows you to override the default window.fetch implementation [1][4]. If you choose to use a custom handler, your handler function is responsible for respecting the options passed to it, including the signal [4][5]. When you call apiFetch with a signal, that option will be included in the options object received by your custom fetch handler [1][4]. If you are implementing a custom handler, ensure your wrapper correctly passes the signal to the underlying fetch or network library you are using [4]. The custom fetch handler acts as the final step in the request chain, executing after all middleware [4].

